Move to mstring:

1. Bridge (now)
Keep both fields. Add two helpers and use only those at the edges:
Cstruct mstring *value_mstring(struct value v);   /* sval → mstring if needed */
const char    *value_cstr(struct value v);       /* mval ? mval->data : sval */

value_cstr is what printf, strlen, and C library calls use.
New code writes mval only.
Old code that still sets sval continues to work.

2. Move the producers
Change the places that create strings, one group at a time, to allocate an mstring and set mval:

The STRING literal production.
value_copy / value_free (refcount on mval; still accept a non-NULL sval during the transition).
Builtins that return strings (first, rest, join, strcat, \ldots).
xfirst one-character string
xrest	copy of tail
xlast	one-char string
xlength	integer character count 
-find : index or -1
xsplit truncates the orginal at index, returns the portion truncated
xappend	concatenate two strings
xinsert	prepend a string to another
-words / split split on whitespace to T_LIST of strings
-join	list of string -> one string, separator optional.

After each group, run the tests. value_print should print mval->data if present, else sval.
3. Move the consumers, then delete
Grep for sval. Each hit is either:

a read → switch to value_cstr(v), or
a write → switch to an mstring allocation into mval.

When the grep is empty, remove sval from struct value and drop the bridge’s sval branch.

- No raw mval / sval outside mstring.c and value_*.  Callers use only:
x mstring_new(const char *s);
x mstring_incref(struct mstring *s);
x mstring_decref(struct mstring *s);
x mstring_cstr(const struct mstring *s);
x mstring_len(const struct mstring *s);
x mstring_trunc(struct mstring *s, int n);   /* split */
x mstring_append(struct mstring *s, const char *extra);


-first one-character string
